You are absolutely right. I used a generic name (PendingBatchDTO) because every project names that file a little differently, but it is very common in Spring Boot applications to simply return the database Entity directly instead of using a DTO at all.
If you cannot find a DTO file, you are likely passing the main database Entity (probably RequestVoucher or whatever entity represents your batch/journal) straight to the frontend.
Here is the foolproof way to find exactly where to put this code, and how to do it without breaking your Oracle database.
### Step 1: Find the right file (The @Transient trick)
Go into your React frontend code and look at the API URL you call to get the pending list (for example, /JS/voucher/pending).
Search your Java project for that URL to find the **Controller**, and look at what type of list it returns.
If it returns something like List<RequestVoucher> or List<JournalBatch>, open that Entity class. You can add the new field directly to the Entity, but you **must** use the @Transient annotation.
**File:** src/main/java/com/fincore/VoucherService/Models/RequestVoucher.java (or whichever entity you found)
```java
import jakarta.persistence.Transient;

// ... inside your Entity class ...

    // @Transient tells Hibernate: "Do NOT look for this column in the Oracle database. 
    // Just hold this data in memory so I can send it to the React frontend."
    @Transient
    private String timeSinceLastAction;

    public String getTimeSinceLastAction() {
        return timeSinceLastAction;
    }

    public void setTimeSinceLastAction(String timeSinceLastAction) {
        this.timeSinceLastAction = timeSinceLastAction;
    }

```
### Step 2: Inject the math into your Service
Now, find the Service method that gets called by that Controller (e.g., getPendingVouchers()).
Since you are returning a list of entities, you just loop through them, run the calculation, and use that new setter before returning the list to the Controller.
**File:** Your Service class (e.g., VoucherQueryService.java)
```java
import java.time.Duration;
import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.util.Optional;

// ... inside your Service class ...

    @Autowired
    private VoucherWorkflowRepository workflowRepository;

    // 1. The helper method
    private String calculateTimeSinceLastAction(String batchId) {
        Optional<VoucherWorkflow> latestAction = 
            workflowRepository.findTopByBatchIdFkOrderByLastActionAtDesc(batchId);

        if (latestAction.isEmpty() || latestAction.get().getLastActionAt() == null) {
            return "0h 0m";
        }

        Duration duration = Duration.between(latestAction.get().getLastActionAt(), LocalDateTime.now());
        return duration.toHours() + "h " + duration.toMinutesPart() + "m";
    }

    // 2. Inside your existing method that gets the pending list:
    public List<RequestVoucher> getPendingList(Long roleId) {
        // ... your existing code that fetches 'pendingList' from the database ...
        
        // Loop through the list and attach the calculated time
        for (RequestVoucher req : pendingList) {
            String time = calculateTimeSinceLastAction(req.getBatchId());
            req.setTimeSinceLastAction(time); 
        }
        
        return pendingList;
    }

```
### Step 3: The React Frontend
Because you used @Transient and the setter in the Service, Spring Boot will automatically include "timeSinceLastAction": "2h 15m" in the JSON it sends to your React app.
You just need to update your table column to read row.timeSinceLastAction exactly as shown in the previous frontend snippet!
